---
import Term from "../Term.astro";
import Try from "../Try.astro";
import "./sternzeit.css";
import LunationSlider from "./LunationSlider.astro";
---

<figure class="moon-figure" id="moon-view">
    <div class="moon-view" data-export="moon" data-download-beside=":scope > .view-toggle">
        <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-field="lockMoon" aria-pressed="true" title="Lock the view to the Moon itself: north up, no horizon">moon frame</button>
        <div class="moon-panel">
            <svg viewBox="-100 -100 200 200" aria-hidden="true"></svg>
            <button type="button" class="view-toggle libration-toggle" data-field="opticalLibration" aria-pressed="true" title="The tilt of the Moon's axis against its orbit and its uneven speed along it let us see a little around its edges">optical libration</button>
        </div>
    </div>
    <LunationSlider />
    <figcaption class="figure-caption">
        The Moon as seen from the chosen place, its north pole (N) up: lit by the Sun, its night side in{" "}
        <Term of="Earthshine">earthshine</Term> (bluer when stronger), the arrow pointing to the Sun. The grid is the
        Moon's own, shifted by the <Term of="Libration">libration</Term>, the dashed circles its size at apogee and
        perigee. Magnified, disc and horizon true to each other. Unlocked from the moon frame, the zenith is up (dashed),
        and the arc is the turn of the north pole against it. The slider runs from full moon to full moon, its marks the
        true phases.
        <Try label="A day and a half after new moon" set="date=2026-11-10T18:00Z" fig="moon-view">
            the thin crescent's night side at its bluest, lit by an almost full Earth.
        </Try>
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<script>
    import "./moonview";
</script>
